Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > connex help output snapshots > connex discover subcommand > connex discover subcommand help column width 120 1`] = `
"▲ vercel connect discover [query] [options]
Discover services you can create a connector for

Matches the query against service names, aliases, keywords, and descriptions.

Options:
  --category <SLUG>             Filter by category. Repeatable. Run \`vercel connect categories\` for valid slugs.
  --connection-method <METHOD>  Filter by connection method (oauth, api-key, mcp, or a service-specific method).
                                Repeatable.
  --json                        Output as JSON
  -n, --limit <COUNT>           Number of services to return (default: 50, max: 500)
  --next <CURSOR>               Show the next page using the cursor from the previous output
  --supports-triggers           Only show services that can forward webhook triggers
  --target <TARGET>             Filter by the product a service exposes (e.g. mcp). Repeatable.

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Discover services
    vercel connect discover

  Search for a service
    vercel connect discover crm

  Filter by category
    vercel connect discover --category ai --category data

  Find services with an MCP server
    vercel connect discover --connection-method mcp

  Find services that support webhook triggers
    vercel connect discover --supports-triggers

  Show the next page, using the cursor from the previous output
    vercel connect discover --next <CURSOR>

  Output as JSON
    vercel connect discover --json

Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > connex help output snapshots > connex help column width 80 1`] = `
"▲ vercel connect <command>
Manage connectors

Commands:
  discover [query]           Discover services you can create a connector for

                             Matches the query against service names, aliases,
                             keywords, and descriptions.
  categories                 List the categories services are grouped into
  create <service>           Create a new connector

                             Accepts a known service (slack, notion, okta) or
                             the URL of an OAuth or MCP server
                             (mcp.notion.com/mcp). Run \`create <service> --help\`
                             to see how a specific service can be connected to.
  update <id>                Update connector branding (icon and colors)
  list                       List connectors linked to the current project
                             (falls back to every connector in the team when no
                             project is linked or when --all-projects is set)
  token <id>                 Get a token for a connector (accepts a connector ID
                             like scl_abc or a UID like slack/my-bot)
  attach <connector>         Attach a Vercel project to a connector for one or
                             more environments
  detach <connector>         Detach a Vercel project from a connector
  delete <connector>         Delete a connector
  revoke-tokens <connector>  Revoke tokens issued from a connector
  open <id>                  Open a connector in the Vercel dashboard
  contacts                   Manage phone contacts for connectors

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Discover services to connect to
    vercel connect discover

  Create a Slack app
    vercel connect create slack

  List connectors on the current team
    vercel connect list

  Get a token
    vercel connect token scl_abc123

  Attach the current project to a connector
    vercel connect attach scl_abc123

  Open a connector in the dashboard
    vercel connect open scl_abc123

Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > connex help output snapshots > connex list subcommand > connex list subcommand help column width 120 1`] = `
"▲ vercel connect list [options]
List connectors linked to the current project (falls back to every connector in the team when no project is linked or
when --all-projects is set)

Options:
  --all-projects       List every connector in the team, regardless of project link
  --json               Output as JSON
  -n, --limit <COUNT>  Number of connectors to return per page
  --next <CURSOR>      Show the next page using the cursor from the previous output
  -q, --query <TEXT>   Search connectors by name or UID
  --service <NAME>     Filter by service name (e.g. slack, mcp.linear.app). Repeatable.
  --type <TYPE>        Filter by connector type (slack, github, oauth, custom). Repeatable.

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  List connectors linked to the current project
    vercel connect list

  List every connector in the team
    vercel connect list --all-projects

  Filter by connector type
    vercel connect list --type slack

  Filter by multiple types
    vercel connect list --type oauth --type github

  Filter by service name
    vercel connect list --service mcp.linear.app

  Search by text
    vercel connect list --query linear

  Combine filters
    vercel connect list --type oauth --query prod

  Limit the number of results
    vercel connect list --limit 10

  Show the next page, using the cursor from the previous output
    vercel connect list --next <CURSOR>

  Output as JSON
    vercel connect list --json

Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > connex help output snapshots > connex open subcommand > connex open subcommand help column width 120 1`] = `
"▲ vercel connect open <id> [options]
Open a connector in the Vercel dashboard

Options:
  --json  Output as JSON

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Open a connector by ID
    vercel connect open scl_abc123

  Open a connector by UID
    vercel connect open slack/my-bot

  Print the dashboard URL as JSON
    vercel connect open scl_abc123 --json

Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > connex help output snapshots > connex token subcommand > connex token subcommand help column width 120 1`] = `
"▲ vercel connect token <id> [options]
Get a token for a connector (accepts a connector ID like scl_abc or a UID like slack/my-bot)

Options:
  --installation <INSTALLATION>  Target a specific installation (only useful with --subject app) (default: the
                                 connector's default installation)
  --json                         Output as JSON
  --no-default-scopes            Do not request the connector default scopes
  --scopes <SCOPES>              Scopes (comma- or space-separated) (default: connector defaults)
  --subject <TYPE>               Subject type: "user" (acts on behalf of you) or "app" (uses the connector's default
                                 installation) (default: "user")
  -y, --yes                      Accept default value for all prompts

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Get a user token by connector ID
    vercel connect token scl_abc123

  Get a token by connector UID
    vercel connect token slack/my-bot

  Get an app token (default installation)
    vercel connect token scl_abc123 --subject app

  Get an app token for a specific installation
    vercel connect token scl_abc123 --subject app --installation inst_1

  Open the browser automatically if authorization/installation is required
    vercel connect token scl_abc123 --yes

  Output as JSON (includes expiresAt, installationId, etc.)
    vercel connect token scl_abc123 --json

Vercel Connect guide:
  https://vercel.com/kb/guide/vercel-connect
"
`;

exports[`help command > deploy help output snapshots > deploy help column width 40 1`] = `
"▲ vercel deploy [project-path] [options]
Deploy your project to Vercel. Use
\`--temporary\` to create a temporary
deployment without logging in. The
\`deploy\` command is the default command
for the Vercel CLI, and can be omitted
(\`vc deploy my-app\` equals \`vc my-app\`).
Use \`--dry\` to inspect the detected
framework preset and source files
without deploying.

Options:
  --archive <FORMAT>
    Compress the deployment code into an
    archive before uploading it
  --build-env <KEY=VALUE>
    Specify environment variables during
    build-time (e.g. \`--build-env
    KEY1=value1 --build-env
    KEY2=value2\`)
  --create-if-missing
    Create the --project if it doesn't
    exist. Required with --yes and in
    non-interactive mode
  --dry
    Inspect the detected framework
    preset and source files without
    uploading or creating a deployment.
    Non-TTY output includes every file
    as JSON
  --env <KEY=VALUE>
    Specify environment variables during
    run-time (e.g. \`--env KEY1=value1
    --env KEY2=value2\`)
  --environment <ENVIRONMENT>
    Environment to deploy to:
    production, preview, or a custom
    environment
  --force
    Force a new deployment even if
    nothing has changed
  --guidance
    Receive command suggestions once
    deployment is complete
  --json
    Output as JSON
  --logs
    Print the build logs
  --meta <KEY=VALUE>
    Specify metadata for the deployment
    (e.g. \`--meta KEY1=value1 --meta
    KEY2=value2\`)
  --no-wait
    Don't wait for the deployment to
    finish
  --prebuilt
    Use in combination with \`vc build\`.
    Deploy an existing build
  --prod
    Create a production deployment
    (shorthand for \`--environment
    production\`)
  -p, --project <NAME_OR_ID>
    Project name or ID to deploy to,
    without linking this directory
    (default: linked project). A missing
    name is created; with --yes or in
    non-interactive mode, add
    --create-if-missing
  --regions <REGION>
    Set default regions to enable the
    deployment on
  --skip-domain
    Disable the automatic promotion
    (aliasing) of the relevant domains
    to a new production deployment. You
    can use \`vc promote\` to complete the
    domain-assignment process later
  --temporary
    Create a temporary deployment
    without logging in
  --turbo
    Use a Turbo build machine for this
    deployment
  --verify
    Load the deployment URL once it is
    ready and report the status code,
    size, and page title, even with
    Deployment Protection on (creates
    the project's Protection Bypass for
    Automation secret if missing). Exits
    1 if the load fails. Can't be used
    with --no-wait or --dry
  --with-cache
    Retain build cache when using
    "--force"
  -y, --yes
    Use default options to skip all
    prompts

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Deploy the current directory
    vercel

  Deploy a single HTML file
    vercel ./index.html

  Deploy a custom path
    vercel /usr/src/project

  Deploy to production and check that
  the URL loads
    vercel deploy --prod --verify

  Deploy with run-time Environment
  Variables
    vercel --env NODE_ENV=production

  Deploy with prebuilt outputs
    vercel build
    vercel deploy --prebuilt

  Deploy using a Turbo build machine
    vercel deploy --turbo

  Inspect deployment inputs without
  deploying
    vercel deploy --dry

  Get every deployment file as JSON
    vercel deploy --dry --json

  Write Deployment URL to a file
    vercel > deployment-url.txt
"
`;

exports[`help command > deploy help output snapshots > deploy help column width 80 1`] = `
"▲ vercel deploy [project-path] [options]
Deploy your project to Vercel. Use \`--temporary\` to create a temporary
deployment without logging in. The \`deploy\` command is the default command for
the Vercel CLI, and can be omitted (\`vc deploy my-app\` equals \`vc my-app\`). Use
\`--dry\` to inspect the detected framework preset and source files without
deploying.

Options:
  --archive <FORMAT>           Compress the deployment code into an archive
                               before uploading it
  --build-env <KEY=VALUE>      Specify environment variables during build-time
                               (e.g. \`--build-env KEY1=value1 --build-env
                               KEY2=value2\`)
  --create-if-missing          Create the --project if it doesn't exist.
                               Required with --yes and in non-interactive mode
  --dry                        Inspect the detected framework preset and source
                               files without uploading or creating a deployment.
                               Non-TTY output includes every file as JSON
  --env <KEY=VALUE>            Specify environment variables during run-time
                               (e.g. \`--env KEY1=value1 --env KEY2=value2\`)
  --environment <ENVIRONMENT>  Environment to deploy to: production, preview, or
                               a custom environment
  --force                      Force a new deployment even if nothing has
                               changed
  --guidance                   Receive command suggestions once deployment is
                               complete
  --json                       Output as JSON
  --logs                       Print the build logs
  --meta <KEY=VALUE>           Specify metadata for the deployment (e.g. \`--meta
                               KEY1=value1 --meta KEY2=value2\`)
  --no-wait                    Don't wait for the deployment to finish
  --prebuilt                   Use in combination with \`vc build\`. Deploy an
                               existing build
  --prod                       Create a production deployment (shorthand for
                               \`--environment production\`)
  -p, --project <NAME_OR_ID>   Project name or ID to deploy to, without linking
                               this directory (default: linked project). A
                               missing name is created; with --yes or in
                               non-interactive mode, add --create-if-missing
  --regions <REGION>           Set default regions to enable the deployment on
  --skip-domain                Disable the automatic promotion (aliasing) of the
                               relevant domains to a new production deployment.
                               You can use \`vc promote\` to complete the
                               domain-assignment process later
  --temporary                  Create a temporary deployment without logging in
  --turbo                      Use a Turbo build machine for this deployment
  --verify                     Load the deployment URL once it is ready and
                               report the status code, size, and page title,
                               even with Deployment Protection on (creates the
                               project's Protection Bypass for Automation secret
                               if missing). Exits 1 if the load fails. Can't be
                               used with --no-wait or --dry
  --with-cache                 Retain build cache when using "--force"
  -y, --yes                    Use default options to skip all prompts

Global Options: See \`vercel --help\`

Examples:
  Deploy the current directory
    vercel

  Deploy a single HTML file
    vercel ./index.html

  Deploy a custom path
    vercel /usr/src/project

  Deploy to production and check that the URL loads
    vercel deploy --prod --verify

  Deploy with run-time Environment Variables
    vercel --env NODE_ENV=production

  Deploy with prebuilt outputs
    vercel build
    vercel deploy --prebuilt

  Deploy using a Turbo build machine
    vercel deploy --turbo

  Inspect deployment inputs without deploying
    vercel deploy --dry

  Get every deployment file as JSON
    vercel deploy --dry --json

  Write Deployment URL to a file
    vercel > deployment-url.txt
"
`;

